          "id": "counterexample",
          "label": "At $x={{testX}}$, what is $r(q(x))$?",
          "inputProfile": "number",
          "expected": "{{composition}}"
        }
      ],
      "solutionReview": {
        "headline": "The unrestricted quadratic is not one-to-one.",
        "reasoning": [
          "Taking the principal square root gives $\\sqrt{(x-{{h}})^2}=|x-{{h}}|$, not $x-{{h}}$ for every real $x$.",
          "At $x={{testX}}$, the composition returns {{composition}}, not {{testX}}.",
          "Restricting the quadratic to $x\\ge {{h}}$ selects the branch that the principal square-root inverse reverses."
        ],
        "answerSummary": "The student's claim fails on the left branch; restrict the quadratic to $x\\ge {{h}}$."
      },
      "attemptFeedback": [
        "The key issue is one-to-one behavior, not whether a square root can be written algebraically."
      ],
      "supportHints": [
        "For any real $u$, $\\sqrt{u^2}=|u|$."
      ],
